(a) The city manager shall deny a temporary use permit if:
(1) The applicant fails to adequately provide for:
a. The protection of the vendors and attendees at the open air market;
b. Maintenance of public order in and around the temporary use location;
c. Crowd security, taking into consideration the size of the market; or
d. Emergency vehicle access.
(2) The applicant fails to comply with or the proposed temporary use will violate a city ordinance or other applicable law, unless the prohibited conduct or activity would be allowed under this chapter;
(3) The applicant makes a false statement of material fact or an application for a temporary use permit or fails to properly complete an application for a market permit;
(4) The applicant has had a temporary use permit revoked within the preceding 12 months;
(5) The applicant fails to pay any outstanding fees assessed under this article for the proposed temporary use for the past open air market;
(6) The chief of the police or fire department or the city manager determines that the temporary use would pose a serious threat to the public health, safety, or welfare;
(7) The applicant or any other person responsible for the conduct or sponsorship of the temporary use is overdue in payment to the city of taxes, fees, fines or penalties assessed against or imposed upon the applicant or other person; or
(8) The applicant fails to acquire or maintain approval from the property owner to operate the temporary use on the property identified in the application.
(b) The city manager shall revoke a temporary use permit for;
(1) The permit holder fails to comply with or the proposed temporary use will violate a city ordinance or other applicable law, unless the prohibited conduct or activity would be allowed under this chapter;
(2) The permit holder makes a false statement of material fact on an application for a temporary use permit or fails to properly complete an application for a market permit;
(3) The chief of the police or fire departments or the city manager determines that the temporary use would pose a serious threat to the public health, safety, or welfare
(4) The applicant fails to pay any outstanding fees assessed under this article for the proposed temporary user for the past open air market.
(5) The permit holder or any other person responsible for the conduct or sponsorship of the temporary use is overdue in payment to the city of taxes, fees, fines or penalties assessedd against or imposed upon the applicant or other person; or
(6) The permit holder fails to acquire or maintain approval from the property owner to operate the temporary use on the property identified in the application
(c) Appeal. If the city manager denies the issuance or renewal of a permit or revokes a permit, the city manager shall send to the applicant or permit holder by certified mail, return receipt requested, written notice of the denial or revocation and of the right to an appeal. The applicant or permit holder may appeal the decision of the city manager to the city council by submitting a written request to the mayor within ten business day after receipt of such denial or revocation. The decision of the city council shall be final.
(Ord. No. 16-05, § 1, 9-19-2016)

